{
  // Type-checks this package's TESTS, which `tsconfig.json` excludes.
  //
  // Why they were excluded, and why that was a hole: `tsconfig.json` is the
  // package BUILD (`tsc` -> dist) with "rootDir": "./src", "composite" and
  // "declaration", so test files would emit into dist. Excluding them keeps the
  // build clean — but it also meant no `tsc` invocation ever read them, and
  // `spec-derived-unions.test.ts` had built its whole contract on `satisfies`
  // checks that therefore never ran. Its header claimed they were "the real
  // enforcement"; reverting a derived alias to its old hand-written fork
  // produced zero errors (objectstack#4074).
  //
  // Same reasoning and same shape as `tsconfig.examples.json`: a separate,
  // emit-free project chained from the package's `type-check` script. Keeping it
  // separate rather than deleting the exclude also keeps the BUILD honest — the
  // "types": ["node"] below is needed by the reexport guard's `readFileSync`
  // source scan, and folding that into `tsconfig.json` would let package SOURCE
  // reference Node APIs and still compile, in a package that ships to browsers.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"],
    // `spec-ui-schema-reexports.test.ts` reads sibling sources off disk to prove
    // no spec/ui zod value hides inside an `export type` block (#2561).
    "types": ["node"],
    // Same reason as tsconfig.json: drop the root tsconfig's source-tree paths
    // so @objectstack/spec resolves through the workspace dependency.
    "paths": {}
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.test.ts", "src/**/*.test.tsx"]
  // The `p1-spec-alignment.test.ts` exclusion that sat here (objectui#3009's
  // declared debt) is gone: its 14 errors were resolved site-by-site in
  // framework#4074 — `ListViewInferred` is `z.input` (authoring surface),
  // `FormField.dependsOn` matches its runtime reader, the normalizer-input
  // dialects are asserted at core's fold seam instead of on this type, and the
  // one fixture no surface ever admitted (an ObjectQL triplet as a tab filter)
  // was corrected.
  //
  // DATED READING — "Every test file in this package is compiled; keep it that
  // way." was TRUE when it was written here on 2026-08-07 (`c35fed098`, PR
  // objectui#3574): the `.dist.spec.tsx` suffix did not yet exist anywhere in the
  // tree. It is kept and dated rather than overwritten, because overwriting it
  // would erase that it was ever true (objectui#9713). It was falsified on
  // 2026-09-09 by `1ccfc235e` (PR objectui#8646), which added this package's
  // first `.dist.spec.tsx` pin. The `include` above did not move — the
  // population under it did.
  //
  // What holds today: every `*.test.ts(x)` file here is compiled, and the
  // `.dist.spec.tsx` built-artifact pins are not. That exclusion is a
  // CONSTRUCTION, not a hole, and a declared exclusion looks exactly like a hole
  // in a directory listing — so read this before you close it. Do NOT widen the
  // `include` above: the suffix keeps those pins out of this program
  // deliberately, because turbo's `type-check` waits on `^build` (the
  // DEPENDENCIES' builds) and must never be handed a program that reads this
  // package's OWN `dist` — objectui#4801 removed a self-referencing `paths`
  // entry for exactly that reason. The pins are still RUN: by the opt-in `dist`
  // vitest project, whose population is `DIST_PIN_GLOB` in the root
  // `vitest.config.mts` (env-gated, reached through `pnpm test:dist`). That
  // constant's docblock carries the full rationale, and objectui#7183 is the card
  // that built the carve-out on purpose.
  //
  // No count is written here, by design: the population moves under this file
  // without touching it, which is how the sentence above rotted.
  // `pnpm type-check:coverage` (`scripts/check-type-check-coverage.mjs`) is what
  // re-derives the claim — it enumerates `*.test.ts(x)` from the package root
  // and resolves this config's `include` / `exclude` to ask whether each one is
  // really read. The pins fall outside that enumerator by the same suffix that
  // keeps them out of the `include`, so the two definitions agree by
  // construction.
}
